\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Schur elimination multiplies quadratic center characters}
\date{2026-10-30}

\p{For an [enriched rational object](fdip-0041), write its
relative precision in the specified orthogonal splitting as
##{Q=\begin{pmatrix}A&B\\B^\dagger&C\end{pmatrix},
\qquad S=A-BC^{-1}B^\dagger.}
The [positive elimination theorem](fdip-000D) gives invertible
positive #{C,S}. The adjoint uses the restricted reference
pairings #{g_{0,\mathrm{ret}},g_{0,\mathrm{el}}}.
Define the two rank-two forms
##{\begin{aligned}
q_S(x)&=g_{0,\mathrm{ret}}(x,Sx),\\
q_C(y)&=g_{0,\mathrm{el}}(y,Cy).
\end{aligned}}
Their signed discriminants and associated quadratic Galois
characters satisfy
##{\delta(q)=\delta(q_S)\delta(q_C),\qquad
\chi_q=\chi_{q_S}\chi_{q_C}.}
Multiplication of the #{\{1,-1\}}-valued characters is their
tensor product. It retains the eliminated character as well
as the retained one.}

\proof{
\p{Put #{K=C^{-1}B^\dagger}, as in \ref{fdip-000D}.
The rational triangular isomorphism
#{U(x,y)=(x,y+Kx)} has determinant one. The
[square-completion identity](fdip-001X) says
##{q(x,y)=q_S(x)+q_C(y+Kx).}
Thus #{q\simeq q_S\perp q_C} as quadratic forms. In a
concatenated rational basis, congruence and #{\det U=1} give
##{\det(b_q)=\det(b_{q_S})\det(b_{q_C}).}
For a rank-two form #{\langle a,b\rangle}, orthogonal
Clifford generators #{e_1,e_2} have squares #{a,b} and
anticommute. Its even algebra has basis #{1,w=e_1e_2},
with #{w^2=-ab}; it is commutative, so it is its own center.
Consequently its center is #{\mathbb Q[z]/(z^2+ab)}, and
its signed discriminant is #{[-ab]}.
The dimension formula and this binary algebra are also
\citet{Section 11, Example 11.2, pp. 57-58}{elmankarpenkomerkurjev2008quadratic}.}

\p{In rank four the signed discriminant has positive determinant
sign by [the field-center calculation](fcap-0043).
The two rank-two minus signs therefore cancel, proving the
first identity. This is the #{2+2} instance of
\citet{Lemma 13.4 and Example 13.5, p. 62}{elmankarpenkomerkurjev2008quadratic}.
Choose square roots of representatives #{d_S,d_C} in
#{\overline{\mathbb Q}}. Their product is a square root of
#{d_Sd_C}; applying any #{\sigma\in G_{\mathbb Q}} gives
##{\frac{\sigma(\sqrt{d_S}\sqrt{d_C})}{\sqrt{d_S}\sqrt{d_C}}
 =\frac{\sigma(\sqrt{d_S})}{\sqrt{d_S}}
  \frac{\sigma(\sqrt{d_C})}{\sqrt{d_C}},}
which proves the character identity, including split classes.}
}

\p{The construction respects the declared arrows. If
#{T=T_{\mathrm{ret}}\oplus T_{\mathrm{el}}} preserves the
pairing, action and splitting, the identity #{Q'T=TQ} gives
##{\begin{aligned}
C'T_{\mathrm{el}}&=T_{\mathrm{el}}C,\\
B'T_{\mathrm{el}}&=T_{\mathrm{ret}}B,\\
(B')^\dagger T_{\mathrm{ret}}&=T_{\mathrm{el}}B^\dagger.
\end{aligned}}
Inversion of the first relation and substitution in the
Schur formula yield
#{S'T_{\mathrm{ret}}=T_{\mathrm{ret}}S}.
The restricted maps consequently preserve #{q_C,q_S} and
both reference pairings. The retained data inherit #{m^2};
they carry no further splitting unless one is supplied.
Thus this naturality statement does not make elimination
an endofunctor on the already split rank-four objects. A
retained trace ratio uses
#{L_{\mathrm{rel,ret}}=S-m^2I} on its own positive-semidefinite,
nonzero-trace domain, with the same trace formula.}

\p{The triangular form isometry generally does not preserve
#{g_0}. Nor does its graph map
##{j_Q(x)=(x,-C^{-1}B^\dagger x)}
preserve the reference pairing: its pulled-back value is
##{g_0(j_Qx,j_Qx')=g_{0,\mathrm{ret}}(x,x')
 +g_{0,\mathrm{el}}(Kx,Kx').}
It is a form isometry #{q(j_Qx)=q_S(x)} into the full space,
not an enriched isomorphism. Clifford functoriality gives
an algebra embedding: after square completion it is the
inclusion of the Clifford algebra of an orthogonal summand,
injective on its monomial basis. But this embedding need
not carry even centers into the full even center. In an
orthogonal basis adapted to #{q_S\perp q_C}, the retained
volume #{e_1e_2} anticommutes with the even element #{e_1f_1},
where #{f_1} is an eliminated nonisotropic generator.
Both products are nonzero, and characteristic is zero;
so this central element of the retained even algebra is
not central in the full even algebra.}

\p{Primitive Dirichlet representatives must be taken after
multiplying Galois characters. They are not in general the
pointwise product of the zero-extended functions on integers.
At #{t=1}, the [cycle Schur computation](fdip-000W) has
#{\det Q=45}, #{\det S=5} and #{C=3I_2}. The three signed
square classes are #{[5],[-5],[-1]}, with fundamental
discriminants #{5,-20,-4}. The Galois product is #{\chi_5},
but
##{\chi_{-20}(2)\chi_{-4}(2)=0,\qquad\chi_5(2)=-1.}
The last value follows from
#{\chi_D(2)=(-1)^{(D^2-1)/8}} for odd fundamental #{D},
as in \citet{Corollary 3.1, p. 4}{conradQuadraticCharacters}.
On integers coprime to twenty the product agrees with
#{\chi_5}; passing to its primitive representative restores
the prime-two value. No multiplication rule for the
corresponding L-functions follows from the character law.
Mathlib's \leanref{DirichletCharacter.primitiveCharacter}
and \leanref{DirichletCharacter.primitiveCharacter_apply_of_isCoprime}
separate primitive reduction from evaluation: agreement is
asserted at integers coprime to the original level, which
does not include the prime two in this example.}
